    ['grammar','A2','If I ___ enough money, I would buy a bicycle.',['had','have','will have','am having'],0,'The second conditional uses if + past simple.'],
    ['vocabulary','B1','A “reliable” source is one you can ___.',['trust','avoid reading','never check','translate automatically'],0,'Reliable means dependable or trustworthy.'],
    ['grammar','B1','She has worked here ___ 2022.',['since','for','during','from'],0,'Since is used with a starting point in time.'],
    ['listening','B1','“I’m afraid we’re fully booked.” What does this mean?',['No places are available','The event is free','The event starts later','Tickets are unlimited'],0,'Fully booked means all places have been reserved.'],
    ['advanced','B1','“Despite the delay, the team finished on time.” What does despite signal?',['A contrast with an expected difficulty','A direct cause','A list of examples','A future condition'],0,'Despite introduces a contrast or obstacle.'],
    ['vocabulary','B1','To “overlook” a detail is to ___.',['fail to notice it','explain it clearly','prove it correct','write it twice'],0,'Overlook can mean fail to notice.'],
    ['grammar','B1','He asked me where I ___.',['lived','do live','am live','will lived'],0,'Reported questions normally use statement word order.'],
    ['vocabulary','B2','A “feasible” plan is ___.',['practical and possible','certain to fail','deliberately unclear','already completed'],0,'Feasible means possible to do successfully.'],
    ['grammar','B2','Had we left earlier, we ___ the traffic.',['would have avoided','will avoid','would avoid','had avoided'],0,'This inverted third conditional refers to an unreal past.'],
    ['listening','B2','“I wouldn’t rule it out.” What does the speaker mean?',['It remains a possibility','It is definitely impossible','It has already happened','It is forbidden by law'],0,'The speaker is not excluding the possibility.'],
    ['advanced','B2','Which phrase expresses cautious agreement?',['That is broadly true, although there are exceptions.','That is always and entirely true.','There is no evidence to consider.','I refuse to discuss it.'],0,'Broadly true signals agreement with qualifications.'],
    ['vocabulary','B2','A “discrepancy” is a ___.',['difference that should be explained','complete agreement','final decision','routine celebration'],0,'A discrepancy is a mismatch or inconsistency.'],
    ['grammar','B2','No sooner ___ the announcement than the phones started ringing.',['had they made','they had made','did they made','they make'],0,'No sooner at the beginning triggers subject–auxiliary inversion.'],
    ['vocabulary','C1','A “tentative” conclusion is ___.',['provisional and open to revision','absolutely certain','unrelated to evidence','intentionally permanent'],0,'Tentative conclusions are not yet final.'],
    ['grammar','C1','Were the evidence stronger, the claim ___ more convincing.',['would be','will have been','is','would have being'],0,'Were + subject is a formal conditional inversion.'],
    ['listening','C1','“Her response was measured rather than dismissive.” She was ___.',['careful and controlled','rude and uninterested','loud and impulsive','completely silent'],0,'Measured means considered and controlled.'],
    ['advanced','C1','Which sentence best avoids overstating causation?',['The findings are consistent with an effect, but do not establish it.','The findings prove every cause beyond doubt.','The result must be causal because it came first.','No further evidence could matter.'],0,'The wording separates consistency with a claim from proof.'],
    ['vocabulary','C1','To “qualify” a statement is to ___.',['limit or make its scope more precise','make it universally true','remove all conditions','repeat it without changes'],0,'Qualifying a statement adds limits or conditions.'],
    ['grammar','C1','Not until the data were checked ___ the error.',['did they discover','they discovered','they did discover','had they discovering'],0,'Fronted negative expressions trigger inversion.'],
    ['vocabulary','C2','“Ineluctable” most nearly means ___.',['impossible to avoid','easy to disprove','openly uncertain','temporarily delayed'],0,'Ineluctable means unavoidable.'],
    ['grammar','C2','Little ___ that the apparently minor change would reshape the debate.',['did they suspect','they suspected','they did suspected','had they suspect'],0,'Little at the beginning triggers inversion.'],
    ['listening','C2','“The proposal is not without merit.” The speaker suggests it ___.',['has some value, despite reservations','is completely worthless','has no drawbacks at all','must be adopted immediately'],0,'This litotes expresses guarded or qualified approval.'],
    ['advanced','C2','Which inference is most defensible from limited evidence?',['The claim remains plausible but unconfirmed.','The claim is certainly true.','The claim is certainly false.','Evidence is unnecessary.'],0,'Limited evidence rarely justifies certainty either way.'],
    ['vocabulary','C2','An “obdurate” person is stubbornly ___.',['unwilling to change their view','eager to compromise','easy to persuade','uncertain about everything'],0,'Obdurate means stubbornly resistant to persuasion.'],
    ['grammar','C2','Only after the audit ___ the full extent of the loss.',['was revealed','the full extent revealed','did the full extent reveal','revealed was'],0,'Only after at the beginning triggers inversion in the main clause.']
